<div style="background-color: #1a5276; color: white; padding: 20px; text-align: center; border-radius: 8px;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0;">Lesson Planner – Theme # The Cat Says 'Meow'. – Lesson # 3</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 3</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Speaking</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> Kinder (CEFR: A1.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> What's that Sound?</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> The Cat Says 'Meow'.</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
            <b>Specific Objective:</b> 
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Speaking: productive: Identify things in simple pictures, using isolated words.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">interactive: Respond to classroom commands.</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2">
            <b>Learning Outcome:</b> 
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Speaking: Can identify animals by their sounds, when supported with pictures, using simple responses (e.g., 'Cats meow.' 'Dogs bark.').</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can imitate animal sounds and gestures during interactive activities (e.g., 'Moo' while pretending to be a cow).</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Can respond to 'What sound?' questions by mimicking the animal sound (e.g., the teacher says, 'What does the cat say?' and the student says, 'Meow!').</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 35%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; background-color: #f2f2f2; width: 15%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students and leads a 'Mystery Animal Sound' game. The teacher plays pre-recorded animal sounds (cat, dog, cow, duck) or makes sounds herself, asking, "What's that sound?". Students point to corresponding flashcards or picture posters and try to say the animal's name and sound (e.g., "Cat! Meow!"). This activates prior knowledge and sets the stage for speaking about animal sounds. Teacher encourages imitation of sounds like /m/ in 'meow' and /r/ in 'rooster' (from previously introduced animals).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a cat. Teacher says clearly: "This is a cat. The cat says 'Meow!'. What does the cat say? Meow!." The teacher makes the 'meow' sound with clear /m/ pronunciation and mimes being a cat. Then, the teacher shows a dog flashcard and says, "This is a dog. The dog says 'Woof!'. What does the dog say? Woof!" The teacher models imitating animal sounds and gestures for each animal, emphasizing the target vocabulary (cat, dog, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig) and simple sentences using "The [animal] says [sound]."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a cat flashcard) "Is this a dog?" (Expected: "No!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">(Showing a cat flashcard) "What sound does the cat say?" (Expected: "Meow!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5-7 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "What's that Sound?")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> Large, colorful picture flashcards of farm and pet animals (cat, dog, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig), animal sound audio clips (if available), and a short, illustrated "Animal Sounds" chart or poster. The material features simple, direct language suitable for Kinder, focusing on identifying animals and their sounds. Genre: Descriptive. Register: Simple, informal. Format: Visual aids and audio.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Pragmatic: Recognizing sounds - Identify common animal sounds during listening activities (e.g., 'moo' for cow) and Sociolinguistic: Describing animal sounds - Identifying and describing different animals, based on their sounds.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher shows a flashcard (e.g., a cow) and plays the sound. Teacher asks, "What animal is this?" and "What sound does it make?" Students respond with isolated words or simple phrases (e.g., "Cow!" "Moo!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Teacher points to different animals on a large poster. Teacher says, "Is this a big dog or a small dog?" (using adjectives 'big' and 'small'). Students point and respond with the adjective.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">8-10 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Accuracy focus on Speaking)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Speaking: productive: Identify things in simple pictures, using isolated words. Speaking: interactive: Respond to classroom commands. Grammar: WH-questions ('What sound is this?'), Present simple ('The cat says meow.'), Exclamations ('That's a rooster!'). Vocabulary: cat, dog, rooster, bird, cow, duck, sheep, horse, pig; say; bark, meow, quack, woof. Pronunciation: /m/ in 'meow', /r/ in 'rooster'. Phonemic awareness: Recognizing letter 'P', 'Q', 'R' in relevant animal names (e.g., Pig, Quail, Rooster – focusing on 'Pig' and 'Rooster' for this lesson).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice – "Picture Says It All":</b> The teacher shows animal flashcards one by one. For each flashcard, the teacher asks, "What is this?" and then "What does the [animal] say?" Students respond chorally or individually with the animal name and its sound (e.g., "It's a cow! It says Moo!"). The teacher specifically guides pronunciation of /m/ in 'meow' and /r/ in 'rooster', and introduces letters 'P' for 'Pig', 'R' for 'Rooster' by showing the letter and pointing to the animal.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice – "Sound Match":</b> The teacher distributes small animal picture cards to each student. The teacher makes an animal sound (e.g., "Woof!"). Students who have the matching animal card (dog) stand up and say, "That's a dog! It says Woof!" or just "Dog! Woof!". This activity encourages students to respond using the target language and associate sound with the animal, responding to the 'What sound?' question by mimicking.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction – "Ask and Tell Animals":</b> Students work in pairs. Each student receives 2-3 animal flashcards. Student A holds up an animal flashcard (e.g., a duck) and asks Student B, "What's this animal?" and "What sound does it say?" Student B responds, "It's a duck! It says Quack!" They then switch roles. The teacher circulates, providing support and correcting pronunciation, ensuring students use the present simple structure "The [animal] says [sound]" and exclamations like "That's a duck!".</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">15-18 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "What's that Sound?")</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> "Animal Sound Show and Tell" - A group/pair role-play activity where students perform for their classmates.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b> Students form small groups (3-4 children). Each group chooses 2-3 animals from the taught vocabulary (e.g., cat, rooster, pig). One student from the group secretly picks an animal card. The other group members pretend to be the animal, making its sound and simple gesture (e.g., for a cat: "Meow! Meow!" and stroking an imaginary head). The rest of the class listens and guesses, "What's that sound?" and "What animal is it?" Once the animal is guessed, the group confirms, "Yes! That's a cat! It says meow!" or "That's a rooster! It says Cock-a-doodle-doo!". The activity focuses on natural interaction, imitating sounds, asking and answering 'What sound?' questions, and identifying animals based on their sounds. Teacher encourages the use of adjectives like 'big' or 'small' if applicable (e.g., "Is it a big cow?").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">10-12 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">(Formative assessment strategy)</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Animal Sound Show and Tell."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students participate in the "Animal Sound Show and Tell" activity, actively mimicking animal sounds and answering "What sound?" and "What animal?" questions in groups.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible pronunciation:</b> Students attempt to pronounce animal names and sounds clearly (e.g., /m/ in 'meow', /r/ in 'rooster').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of target vocabulary:</b> Students use animal names (cat, dog, cow, duck, pig, rooster) and their sounds (meow, bark, moo, quack, oink, cock-a-doodle-doo).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of response:</b> Students give simple, understandable responses (e.g., "Cat!", "Meow!", "That's a dog!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration/Interaction:</b> Students participate actively in group guessing and responding.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> The teacher asks students: "Did you like making animal sounds today?" (Thumbs up/down). "Can you make a cat sound?" (Students attempt 'meow').</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was your favorite animal sound to make today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can you say 'P' for Pig or 'R' for Rooster?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; padding-left: 20px; margin-top: 0; margin-bottom: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The "Animal Sound Show and Tell" activity worked very well to promote speaking and interaction. Most students were able to identify animals and make their sounds. The /m/ and /r/ sounds were practiced effectively. Some students still need more practice with full sentence responses like "The cat says meow" and connecting 'P' and 'R' with animal names.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Adjustments needed: For quieter students, provide more individual prompts and positive reinforcement. Ensure all students get a chance to hold a flashcard and speak during practice activities. Scaffold the WH-questions more explicitly if needed by providing sentence starters.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson built strong oral foundations for identifying animals and their sounds. The next lesson (Lesson 4: Writing Composition) will focus on reinforcing these animal names and sounds in a written format, using simple sentences and perhaps tracing activities, building on the phonemic awareness practiced today.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">5 min</td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>